 prompt names but the zip lacks

INVARIANT PROTECTED: the operator agent has tools read / hash / measure /
report / refuse - and no others.

Finding: MASTER_PROMPT_FAIL_CLOSED.md and SLICE78_CERTIFICATION.md state
that tools/operator_paper_daily.py, tools/connector_check.py and
tools/append_closed_corpus.py exist in the tree and were executed on
2026-09-01. `ls tools | grep -E "operator_paper_daily|connector_check|
append_closed"` on the uploaded zip is empty. The slice-77/78 artefacts
carry git commits (f890d32, bf9bcfd, 6da4582) that are not this tree.
Everything those certificates claim about these scripts is therefore
unverifiable from the uploaded bytes. This patch makes the scripts exist,
with the properties the prompt demands, and pins each property in a test.

operator_paper_daily.py  one-shot; exit 2 on USE_TESTNET=0+PAPER_TRADING=0
  BEFORE reading anything; exit 3 if LIVE_TRADING_ACK is set in a paper
  run, models/current exists, or the state DB's kill switch is engaged
  (the shipped fixture DB is engaged: "certification test"); prints
  project_status, the gate verdict re-derived by promotion_gate, and the
  6-rung ladder from the NEWEST artifacts/slice*_forward_shadow.json.
  Always reports closer_to_autonomous_profit_agent=False because that is
  a derived fact, not a field a tool may set.

connector_check.py  five public GETs, no keys, no orders; verdicts
  BYBIT_TESTNET_BLOCKED / CORPUS_PATH_OK; venue_changed is always False.
  From this sandbox every endpoint is unreachable (egress allow-list) -
  which is precisely the case the tool exists to report.

append_closed_corpus.py  BTCUSDT + Binance USDT-M only. A bar is
  appended iff closeTime < observed_at (the open bar is dropped by
  arithmetic, not by trusting the endpoint); funding prints only at-or-
  before observed_at; UTC-midnight alignment and gap/overlap checks
  refuse; --expect-*-sha256 refuses on mismatch BEFORE any write;
  historical bytes are asserted to be a byte prefix; CRLF preserved;
  dry-run by default; before/after sha256 published. Fetch is injectable
  so the closed-bar logic is unit-tested without network.

Verified against the uploaded corpus: linear sha256 293774ee... and
funding sha256 c652c9b0... equal the "before" digests in
SLICE78_CERTIFICATION.md, so the corpus in this zip IS the slice-76
corpus those certificates started from.

Tests: tests/test_operator_tools.py, 11 tests. Row FORMATTING of new
bars (e.g. "77719" vs "77719.0") cannot be proven identical to whatever
produced the historical rows - CANNOT DETERMINE without the original
fetcher; the prefix invariant does not depend on it.
